        "chapterNumber": 21,
        "title": "Synonyms: Words with Similar Meanings",
        "excerpt": "Learn how to identify synonyms, understand shades of meaning and choose the most appropriate replacement for words in JAMB examination questions.",
        "readTimeMinutes": 25,
        "content": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Vocabulary is an important part of English Language. In JAMB Use of English, you may be asked to select a word that has the same or nearly the same meaning as another word. Such words are called synonyms.</p><p>Understanding synonyms improves your reading, writing, comprehension and ability to answer vocabulary questions correctly.</p><h2>1. What Is a Synonym?</h2><p>A synonym is a word that has the same or nearly the same meaning as another word. Two words are synonyms when they can express similar ideas in an appropriate context.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Happy — joyful</li><li>Angry — furious</li><li>Begin — commence</li><li>End — finish</li><li>Brave — courageous</li><li>Wealthy — rich</li><li>Rapid — swift</li><li>Assist — help</li></ul><p>Synonyms do not always have exactly the same meaning. Their suitability may depend on the sentence in which they are used.</p><h2>2. Types of Synonyms</h2><h3>A. Complete or Near-Complete Synonyms</h3><p>Some words have almost the same meaning in many situations.</p><p>Examples: begin/start, end/finish, answer/reply and assist/help.</p><h3>B. Contextual Synonyms</h3><p>Some words are similar only in particular contexts. For example, 'bright' may mean intelligent in 'a bright student', but it may mean shining in 'a bright light'. The correct synonym depends on the context.</p><h3>C. Formal and Informal Synonyms</h3><p>Some synonyms differ in level of formality.</p><ul><li>Child — kid (informal)</li><li>Purchase — buy (purchase is more formal)</li><li>Commence — start (commence is more formal)</li><li>Residence — home (residence is more formal)</li></ul><h2>3. Common Synonym Groups for JAMB</h2><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Possible synonym</th></tr><tr><td>Abandon</td><td>Desert, forsake</td></tr><tr><td>Abundant</td><td>Plentiful, ample</td></tr><tr><td>Accurate</td><td>Correct, precise</td></tr><tr><td>Admire</td><td>Respect, appreciate</td></tr><tr><td>Adversity</td><td>Hardship, difficulty</td></tr><tr><td>Ancient</td><td>Old, antique</td></tr><tr><td>Anxious</td><td>Worried, uneasy</td></tr><tr><td>Artificial</td><td>Man-made, synthetic</td></tr><tr><td>Audacious</td><td>Bold, daring</td></tr><tr><td>Benevolent</td><td>Kind, charitable</td></tr><tr><td>Candid</td><td>Frank, honest</td></tr><tr><td>Cease</td><td>Stop, discontinue</td></tr><tr><td>Conceal</td><td>Hide, cover</td></tr><tr><td>Conspicuous</td><td>Noticeable, obvious</td></tr><tr><td>Diligent</td><td>Hardworking, industrious</td></tr><tr><td>Dispute</td><td>Argument, disagreement</td></tr><tr><td>Elated</td><td>Delighted, overjoyed</td></tr><tr><td>Enormous</td><td>Huge, gigantic</td></tr><tr><td>Feeble</td><td>Weak, frail</td></tr><tr><td>Hostile</td><td>Unfriendly, antagonistic</td></tr><tr><td>Impartial</td><td>Fair, unbiased</td></tr><tr><td>Imminent</td><td>Approaching, impending</td></tr><tr><td>Lucid</td><td>Clear, understandable</td></tr><tr><td>Meticulous</td><td>Careful, thorough</td></tr><tr><td>Obsolete</td><td>Outdated, old-fashioned</td></tr><tr><td>Obstinate</td><td>Stubborn, headstrong</td></tr><tr><td>Peril</td><td>Danger, risk</td></tr><tr><td>Reluctant</td><td>Unwilling, hesitant</td></tr><tr><td>Resilient</td><td>Strong, able to recover</td></tr><tr><td>Vague</td><td>Unclear, indefinite</td></tr></table><h2>4. Synonyms Must Fit the Context</h2><p>One of the most important rules in JAMB vocabulary questions is that a word must be interpreted according to the sentence in which it appears.</p><p>Consider the word 'sharp':</p><ul><li>The knife is sharp. (Having a cutting edge)</li><li>She has a sharp mind. (Intelligent or quick to understand)</li><li>He felt a sharp pain. (Sudden or intense)</li><li>The bus made a sharp turn. (Sudden or abrupt)</li></ul><p>A single synonym cannot replace 'sharp' correctly in all four sentences.</p><h2>5. How to Answer Synonym Questions</h2><ol><li>Read the entire sentence or question carefully.</li><li>Identify the word being tested.</li><li>Determine the meaning of the word in that particular context.</li><li>Check the grammatical function of the word. A noun should normally be replaced with a noun, a verb with a verb, and so on.</li><li>Compare the options and eliminate words with opposite or unrelated meanings.</li><li>Choose the closest suitable meaning, not merely a word that looks familiar.</li></ol><h2>6. Common JAMB Examination Traps</h2><ul><li>Two words may be synonyms in one context but not in another.</li><li>Do not select an answer simply because it is a more advanced or impressive word.</li><li>Check whether the replacement preserves the meaning of the sentence.</li><li>Watch out for words with more than one meaning, such as 'light', 'grave', 'fine' and 'mean'.</li><li>Some options may be related to the question word without being true synonyms.</li></ul><h2>7. Worked JAMB-Style Questions</h2><p><b>Question 1:</b> The chairman gave a <b>candid</b> explanation of the incident. Choose the word nearest in meaning to the word in bold.</p><ol type='A'><li>Confusing</li><li>Frank</li><li>Unnecessary</li><li>Careless</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Frank.</b> A candid explanation is honest and direct.</p><p><b>Question 2:</b> The workers were <b>reluctant</b> to accept the new conditions.</p><ol type='A'><li>Willing</li><li>Unwilling</li><li>Excited</li><li>Prepared</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Unwilling.</b> Reluctant means hesitant or unwilling to do something.</p><p><b>Question 3:</b> The manager was praised for his <b>meticulous</b> attention to detail.</p><ol type='A'><li>Careless</li><li>Thorough</li><li>Quick</li><li>Unusual</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Thorough.</b> Meticulous means extremely careful and attentive to details.</p><p><b>Question 4:</b> The old computer system has become <b>obsolete</b>.</p><ol type='A'><li>Modern</li><li>Expensive</li><li>Outdated</li><li>Powerful</li></ol><p><b>Answer: C. Outdated.</b> Obsolete describes something that is no longer useful or commonly used because it has been replaced.</p><h2>8. Revision Points</h2><ul><li>A synonym is a word with the same or nearly the same meaning as another word.</li><li>Context determines the most suitable synonym.</li><li>Consider the grammatical function of the word.</li><li>Eliminate options that are opposites or merely related words.</li><li>Read the entire sentence before choosing an answer.</li></ul><h2>9. Self-Assessment</h2><ol><li>Choose a synonym for 'abundant'.</li><li>What is the closest meaning of 'hostile'?</li><li>Give two synonyms for 'diligent'.</li><li>Explain why 'bright' may have different synonyms in different sentences.</li><li>Choose the word nearest in meaning to 'imminent': distant, approaching, completed or impossible.</li><li>What does 'lucid' mean?</li><li>Give a synonym for 'peril'.</li></ol>"
