        "title": "Antonyms: Words with Opposite Meanings",
        "excerpt": "Learn how to identify opposite meanings, distinguish contextual antonyms and avoid confusing similar-looking vocabulary options.",
        "readTimeMinutes": 24,
        "content": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>English contains words that express opposite ideas. Understanding these words helps you communicate clearly and answer vocabulary questions in JAMB Use of English.</p><h2>1. What Is an Antonym?</h2><p>An antonym is a word that has an opposite or contrasting meaning to another word.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Hot — cold</li><li>Rich — poor</li><li>Accept — reject</li><li>Victory — defeat</li><li>Generous — selfish</li><li>Ancient — modern</li></ul><h2>2. Types of Antonyms</h2><h3>A. Gradable Antonyms</h3><p>These are opposites that exist along a scale. There may be degrees between them.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Hot — cold (warm and cool exist between them)</li><li>Big — small (medium-sized exists between them)</li><li>Happy — sad (other emotional states exist between them)</li><li>Rich — poor (different levels of wealth exist)</li></ul><h3>B. Complementary Antonyms</h3><p>These are pairs in which one condition excludes the other in the intended sense. There is normally no middle position.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Alive — dead</li><li>Present — absent</li><li>True — false</li><li>Pass — fail (in a clearly defined examination result)</li></ul><h3>C. Relational or Converse Antonyms</h3><p>These words describe the same relationship from opposite positions.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Buy — sell</li><li>Teacher — student</li><li>Parent — child</li><li>Employer — employee</li><li>Above — below</li></ul><p>A person who buys something is involved in the same transaction as the person who sells it, but from the opposite side.</p><h2>3. Common Antonym Groups for JAMB</h2><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Antonym</th></tr><tr><td>Abundant</td><td>Scarce</td></tr><tr><td>Accept</td><td>Reject</td></tr><tr><td>Accurate</td><td>Inaccurate</td></tr><tr><td>Advance</td><td>Retreat</td></tr><tr><td>Ancient</td><td>Modern</td></tr><tr><td>Artificial</td><td>Natural</td></tr><tr><td>Arrogant</td><td>Humble</td></tr><tr><td>Bold</td><td>Timid</td></tr><tr><td>Bravery</td><td>Cowardice</td></tr><tr><td>Construct</td><td>Destroy</td></tr><tr><td>Conceal</td><td>Reveal</td></tr><tr><td>Expand</td><td>Contract</td></tr><tr><td>Flexible</td><td>Rigid</td></tr><tr><td>Generous</td><td>Selfish</td></tr><tr><td>Genuine</td><td>Fake</td></tr><tr><td>Hostile</td><td>Friendly</td></tr><tr><td>Humble</td><td>Proud</td></tr><tr><td>Include</td><td>Exclude</td></tr><tr><td>Innocent</td><td>Guilty</td></tr><tr><td>Optimistic</td><td>Pessimistic</td></tr><tr><td>Permanent</td><td>Temporary</td></tr><tr><td>Plentiful</td><td>Scarce</td></tr><tr><td>Polite</td><td>Rude</td></tr><tr><td>Prudent</td><td>Reckless</td></tr><tr><td>Rigid</td><td>Flexible</td></tr><tr><td>Transparent</td><td>Opaque</td></tr><tr><td>Triumph</td><td>Defeat</td></tr><tr><td>Vacant</td><td>Occupied</td></tr><tr><td>Virtue</td><td>Vice</td></tr><tr><td>Wisdom</td><td>Foolishness</td></tr></table><h2>4. Antonyms in Context</h2><p>Just as with synonyms, antonyms must be selected according to the meaning of the word in the sentence.</p><p>Consider 'light':</p><ul><li>This bag is light. Opposite: heavy.</li><li>The room is light. Opposite: dark.</li><li>He gave me a light meal. Opposite in context: heavy or substantial.</li></ul><p>The correct opposite depends on how the word is used.</p><h2>5. Negative Prefixes</h2><p>Some antonyms are formed by adding prefixes that reverse or negate the meaning of a word.</p><table><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Un-</td><td>Happy/unhappy; fair/unfair</td></tr><tr><td>In-</td><td>Accurate/inaccurate; complete/incomplete</td></tr><tr><td>Im-</td><td>Possible/impossible; patient/impatient</td></tr><tr><td>Il-</td><td>Legal/illegal; logical/illogical</td></tr><tr><td>Ir-</td><td>Regular/irregular; responsible/irresponsible</td></tr><tr><td>Dis-</td><td>Agree/disagree; honest/dishonest</td></tr><tr><td>Non-</td><td>Violent/non-violent; payment/non-payment</td></tr></table><p>Not every word can take any negative prefix. Learn the correct spelling and conventional form.</p><h2>6. How to Answer Antonym Questions</h2><ol><li>Read the sentence and identify the tested word.</li><li>Determine its exact meaning in that context.</li><li>Think of its opposite before looking at the options.</li><li>Check whether the options preserve the same grammatical function.</li><li>Choose the most direct opposite, not merely a word that expresses a different idea.</li></ol><h2>7. Common JAMB Examination Traps</h2><ul><li>Some words have more than one meaning and therefore more than one possible opposite.</li><li>A word that is unrelated to another word is not necessarily its antonym.</li><li>Be careful with negative prefixes. For example, 'inflammable' does not mean non-flammable; it means capable of catching fire.</li><li>Some pairs express different sides of a relationship rather than simple opposites.</li><li>Check the sentence context before choosing an answer.</li></ul><h2>8. Worked JAMB-Style Questions</h2><p><b>Question 1:</b> The chairman was praised for his <b>generosity</b>. Choose the word opposite in meaning to the word in bold.</p><ol type='A'><li>Kindness</li><li>Selfishness</li><li>Honesty</li><li>Patience</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Selfishness.</b> Generosity means willingness to give or share; selfishness is its opposite.</p><p><b>Question 2:</b> The supply of food was <b>abundant</b> throughout the festival.</p><ol type='A'><li>Plentiful</li><li>Excessive</li><li>Scarce</li><li>Available</li></ol><p><b>Answer: C. Scarce.</b> Abundant means plentiful, while scarce means insufficient or difficult to find.</p><p><b>Question 3:</b> The officer gave a <b>rigid</b> instruction.</p><ol type='A'><li>Strict</li><li>Flexible</li><li>Firm</li><li>Fixed</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Flexible.</b> In this context, rigid means inflexible or not easily changed.</p><p><b>Question 4:</b> The witness was declared <b>innocent</b>.</p><ol type='A'><li>Honest</li><li>Guilty</li><li>Reliable</li><li>Careless</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Guilty.</b> Innocent and guilty express opposite legal or moral conditions.</p><h2>9. Revision Points</h2><ul><li>An antonym is a word with an opposite or contrasting meaning.</li><li>Gradable antonyms allow degrees between them.</li><li>Complementary antonyms represent mutually exclusive conditions.</li><li>Relational antonyms describe opposite sides of a relationship.</li><li>Context determines the correct opposite.</li></ul><h2>10. Self-Assessment</h2><ol><li>Give the opposite of 'optimistic'.</li><li>What is the antonym of 'conceal'?</li><li>Explain the difference between gradable and complementary antonyms.</li><li>Give the opposite of 'transparent'.</li><li>Choose the antonym of 'prudent': careful, wise, reckless or sensible.</li><li>Give an antonym of 'temporary'.</li><li>Explain why 'light' may have different antonyms.</li></ol>"
