        "content": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Some English words look alike or sound alike but have different meanings. These words can be confusing, especially in written examinations. JAMB may test your understanding of their meanings, spelling and use in sentences.</p><h2>1. What Are Homonyms?</h2><p>Homonyms are words that share the same form or pronunciation but have different meanings. In common school grammar, the term is often used broadly for words that are either homophones or homographs.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Bank: a financial institution; the side of a river.</li><li>Bat: an animal that flies at night; a piece of equipment used in sports.</li><li>Match: a contest; a small stick used to light a fire.</li><li>Ring: a piece of jewellery; the sound made by a bell or telephone.</li></ul><h2>2. Homophones</h2><p>Homophones are words that have the same or nearly the same pronunciation but different meanings. They are often spelt differently.</p><p>Examples:</p><table><tr><th>Word 1</th><th>Word 2</th><th>Difference</th></tr><tr><td>There</td><td>Their</td><td>Place/existence versus possession</td></tr><tr><td>To</td><td>Too</td><td>Direction/infinitive marker versus also/excessively</td></tr><tr><td>Right</td><td>Write</td><td>Correct/direction versus put words on paper</td></tr><tr><td>Hear</td><td>Here</td><td>Perceive sound versus this place</td></tr><tr><td>Son</td><td>Sun</td><td>Male child versus the star that gives Earth light</td></tr><tr><td>Sea</td><td>See</td><td>Large body of salt water versus perceive with the eyes</td></tr><tr><td>Flour</td><td>Flower</td><td>Powder used in baking versus a plant bloom</td></tr><tr><td>Pair</td><td>Pear</td><td>Two matching items versus a fruit</td></tr><tr><td>Week</td><td>Weak</td><td>Seven-day period versus lacking strength</td></tr><tr><td>Peace</td><td>Piece</td><td>Absence of conflict versus a portion</td></tr><tr><td>Brake</td><td>Break</td><td>Device for stopping a vehicle versus damage or pause</td></tr><tr><td>Allowed</td><td>Aloud</td><td>Permitted versus spoken audibly</td></tr><tr><td>Principal</td><td>Principle</td><td>Head of a school or main person versus a rule or belief</td></tr><tr><td>Stationary</td><td>Stationery</td><td>Not moving versus writing materials</td></tr><tr><td>Weather</td><td>Whether</td><td>Atmospheric conditions versus expressing alternatives</td></tr></table><h2>3. Homographs</h2><p>Homographs are words that have the same spelling but different meanings. They may have the same pronunciation or different pronunciations.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li><b>Lead:</b> to guide; a type of metal. The verb is pronounced differently from the noun for the metal.</li><li><b>Tear:</b> liquid from the eye; to rip something. These have different pronunciations.</li><li><b>Wind:</b> moving air; to turn or twist. These have different pronunciations.</li><li><b>Object:</b> a thing; to express disagreement. The noun and verb have different stress patterns.</li><li><b>Record:</b> a written or stored account; to capture information. The noun and verb commonly differ in stress.</li></ul><h2>4. Words with the Same Form but Different Meanings</h2><p>Some words have several meanings depending on how they are used.</p><ul><li><b>Fair:</b> just; light in colour; a public exhibition or event.</li><li><b>Fine:</b> good or satisfactory; a payment for breaking a rule; very thin or delicate.</li><li><b>Mean:</b> intend; signify; unkind; an average in mathematics.</li><li><b>Light:</b> illumination; not heavy; pale in colour.</li><li><b>Right:</b> correct; a direction; an entitlement.</li></ul><h2>5. Why These Words Matter in JAMB</h2><p>Questions may test whether you understand the meaning of a word in context, whether you can choose the correct spelling, or whether you can distinguish two words that sound alike.</p><p>Example: 'The school principal explained an important principle of discipline.' The first word refers to the head of the school; the second refers to a rule or fundamental idea.</p><h2>6. Commonly Confused Words</h2><h3>A. Advice and Advise</h3><p>Advice is a noun meaning guidance. Advise is a verb meaning to give guidance.</p><ul><li>My father gave me useful advice.</li><li>My father advised me to study harder.</li></ul><h3>B. Affect and Effect</h3><p>Affect is usually a verb meaning to influence. Effect is usually a noun meaning a result.</p><ul><li>The rain affected the journey.</li><li>The medicine had a positive effect.</li></ul><h3>C. Loose and Lose</h3><p>Loose is an adjective meaning not tight. Lose is a verb meaning to misplace or fail to win.</p><ul><li>This shirt is too loose.</li><li>Do not lose your examination slip.</li></ul><h3>D. Then and Than</h3><p>Then relates to time or sequence. Than is used in comparisons.</p><ul><li>We ate and then went home.</li><li>Mary is taller than Ada.</li></ul><h3>E. Your and You're</h3><p>Your shows possession. You're is a contraction of 'you are'.</p><ul><li>Your book is on the table.</li><li>You're going to enjoy the lesson.</li></ul><h2>7. How to Avoid Confusing These Words</h2><ol><li>Learn the meaning of each word, not just its pronunciation.</li><li>Use the word in a complete sentence.</li><li>Check whether the word should be a noun, verb or adjective.</li><li>Pay attention to spelling differences.</li><li>Read the whole sentence before selecting an answer.</li></ol><h2>8. Worked JAMB-Style Questions</h2><p><b>Question 1:</b> Choose the correct word: The school ______ addressed the students during assembly.</p><ol type='A'><li>principle</li><li>principal</li><li>principles</li><li>principals</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Principal.</b> The sentence refers to the head of the school.</p><p><b>Question 2:</b> The teacher gave the students useful ______ before the examination.</p><ol type='A'><li>advise</li><li>advice</li><li>advises</li><li>advising</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Advice.</b> The sentence requires a noun meaning guidance.</p><p><b>Question 3:</b> The new policy may ______ the cost of transportation.</p><ol type='A'><li>effect</li><li>affect</li><li>effects</li><li>affects</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Affect.</b> The sentence needs a verb meaning influence.</p><p><b>Question 4:</b> Choose the correct word: The bag is too ______ to carry comfortably.</p><ol type='A'><li>lose</li><li>loose</li><li>loss</li><li>loosely</li></ol><p><b>Answer: B. Loose.</b> The adjective 'loose' describes something that is not tight or secure.</p><h2>9. Revision Points</h2><ul><li>Homophones sound alike but have different meanings and often different spellings.</li><li>Homographs have the same spelling but different meanings.</li><li>Homonyms is a broad term for words sharing form or pronunciation while having different meanings.</li><li>Context helps determine the correct meaning and spelling.</li><li>Advice/advise, affect/effect, loose/lose and principal/principle are common sources of errors.</li></ul><h2>10. Self-Assessment</h2><ol><li>Differentiate between homophones and homographs.</li><li>Choose between 'principal' and 'principle': The school ______ announced the new rules.</li><li>Choose between 'advice' and 'advise': I need your ______.</li><li>Choose between 'affect' and 'effect': The flood had a serious ______ on the community.</li><li>Give two meanings of the word 'bank'.</li><li>Explain the difference between 'stationary' and 'stationery'.</li><li>Complete: You are taller ______ your brother.</li></ol>"
