        "title": "English Vowel Sounds: Monophthongs and Diphthongs",
        "excerpt": "Understand English vowel sounds, learn the difference between monophthongs and diphthongs, and practise identifying vowel sounds in JAMB Oral English questions.",
        "readTimeMinutes": 35,
        "content": "<h2>1. Introduction to Vowel Sounds</h2><p>Vowel sounds are speech sounds produced when air flows freely through the mouth without being blocked by the tongue, teeth or lips. The main English vowel letters are a, e, i, o and u, but the English language has more vowel sounds than these five letters.</p><p>This means that a single vowel letter can represent different sounds, while different combinations of letters can produce the same sound.</p><p>For example, the letter 'a' has different pronunciations in cat, cake and father. Oral English questions therefore test sounds, not merely spelling.</p><h2>2. Vowel Letters and Vowel Sounds</h2><p>Do not confuse vowel letters with vowel sounds. A vowel letter is a written symbol, while a vowel sound is what you actually pronounce.</p><p>For example:</p><ul><li>Cat contains the vowel sound /æ/.</li><li>Cake contains the vowel sound /eɪ/.</li><li>Father contains the vowel sound /ɑː/.</li><li>About begins with the vowel sound /ə/.</li></ul><p>The International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) uses symbols to represent speech sounds. Learning these symbols helps you answer pronunciation questions accurately.</p><h2>3. Monophthongs</h2><p>A monophthong is a vowel sound produced with one steady sound quality. The tongue and mouth position remain relatively stable while the sound is produced. It is also called a pure vowel.</p><p>English monophthongs are commonly divided into short vowels and long vowels.</p><h3>A. Short Vowel Sounds</h3><table><tr><th>IPA symbol</th><th>Example words</th><th>Pronunciation guide</th></tr><tr><td>/ɪ/</td><td>sit, ship, little</td><td>Short sound in sit</td></tr><tr><td>/e/</td><td>bed, pen, head</td><td>Sound in bed</td></tr><tr><td>/æ/</td><td>cat, bag, man</td><td>Sound in cat</td></tr><tr><td>/ʌ/</td><td>cup, bus, love</td><td>Sound in cup</td></tr><tr><td>/ɒ/</td><td>hot, pot, clock</td><td>Sound in hot in many British accents</td></tr><tr><td>/ʊ/</td><td>book, put, good</td><td>Sound in book</td></tr><tr><td>/ə/</td><td>about, teacher, ago</td><td>Weak sound in about</td></tr></table><h3>B. Long Vowel Sounds</h3><table><tr><th>IPA symbol</th><th>Example words</th><th>Pronunciation guide</th></tr><tr><td>/iː/</td><td>see, seat, green</td><td>Long ee sound</td></tr><tr><td>/ɑː/</td><td>car, father, calm</td><td>Long ah sound in many British pronunciations</td></tr><tr><td>/ɔː/</td><td>law, saw, thought</td><td>Long aw sound in accents that distinguish it</td></tr><tr><td>/uː/</td><td>food, blue, school</td><td>Long oo sound</td></tr><tr><td>/ɜː/</td><td>bird, nurse, learn</td><td>Long central vowel in many British pronunciations</td></tr></table><p><strong>Important:</strong> The exact pronunciation of some words differs between British, Nigerian and other varieties of English. For JAMB, pay attention to the pronunciation model used in your prescribed examination materials.</p><h2>4. Diphthongs</h2><p>A diphthong is a vowel sound that involves a movement from one vowel position towards another within the same syllable. Unlike a monophthong, it does not remain at one steady vowel position.</p><table><tr><th>IPA symbol</th><th>Example words</th></tr><tr><td>/eɪ/</td><td>day, make, rain</td></tr><tr><td>/aɪ/</td><td>my, time, light</td></tr><tr><td>/ɔɪ/</td><td>boy, coin, choice</td></tr><tr><td>/əʊ/</td><td>go, home, road</td></tr><tr><td>/aʊ/</td><td>now, house, brown</td></tr><tr><td>/ɪə/</td><td>near, here, fear</td></tr><tr><td>/eə/</td><td>care, hair, chair</td></tr><tr><td>/ʊə/</td><td>pure, tourist, during in some traditional British pronunciations</td></tr></table><p>Some modern English accents pronounce certain traditional diphthongs differently. The important examination skill is recognising the sound pattern used in the question.</p><h2>5. Monophthong Versus Diphthong</h2><table><tr><th>Monophthong</th><th>Diphthong</th></tr><tr><td>One steady vowel sound.</td><td>A movement between two vowel positions.</td></tr><tr><td>The tongue position remains relatively stable.</td><td>The tongue moves during pronunciation.</td></tr><tr><td>Example: /ɪ/ in sit.</td><td>Example: /aɪ/ in site.</td></tr><tr><td>Example: /e/ in bed.</td><td>Example: /eɪ/ in bait.</td></tr></table><h2>6. Common Vowel Sound Contrasts</h2><p>JAMB may present words that look similar but contain different vowel sounds.</p><table><tr><th>Word pair</th><th>First sound</th><th>Second sound</th></tr><tr><td>Ship / sheep</td><td>/ɪ/</td><td>/iː/</td></tr><tr><td>Full / fool</td><td>/ʊ/</td><td>/uː/</td></tr><tr><td>Hat / heart</td><td>/æ/</td><td>/ɑː/ in many British accents</td></tr><tr><td>Bed / bad</td><td>/e/</td><td>/æ/</td></tr><tr><td>Cut / cart</td><td>/ʌ/</td><td>/ɑː/ in many British accents</td></tr></table><h2>7. The Schwa Sound /ə/</h2><p>The schwa is a weak vowel sound commonly found in unstressed syllables. It is represented by /ə/.</p><p>Examples include:</p><ul><li>About: the first vowel sound.</li><li>Teacher: the final weak vowel sound in many pronunciations.</li><li>Doctor: the final weak vowel sound in many pronunciations.</li><li>Banana: the first and final weak vowel sounds in common pronunciations.</li></ul><p>The schwa is important because English speakers often reduce unstressed vowels. A word may therefore sound different from what its spelling suggests.</p><h2>8. Worked JAMB-Style Questions</h2><h3>Question 1</h3><p>Which word contains the same vowel sound as 'ship'?</p><ol type='A'><li>Sheep</li><li>Bit</li><li>Seat</li><li>Leave</li></ol><p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bit.</strong> Ship and bit contain /ɪ/.</p><h3>Question 2</h3><p>Which word contains the diphthong /aɪ/?</p><ol type='A'><li>Seat</li><li>Boy</li><li>Time</li><li>Book</li></ol><p><strong>Correct answer: C. Time.</strong> Time contains the diphthong /aɪ/.</p><h3>Question 3</h3><p>Which of the following contains the vowel sound /uː/?</p><ol type='A'><li>Foot</li><li>Food</li><li>Book</li><li>Good</li></ol><p><strong>Correct answer: B. Food.</strong> Food contains the long vowel /uː/.</p><h3>Question 4</h3><p>Which statement correctly describes a monophthong?</p><ol type='A'><li>It contains two consonants.</li><li>It is a steady vowel sound.</li><li>It always occurs at the end of a word.</li><li>It is produced by completely blocking the airflow.</li></ol><p><strong>Correct answer: B.</strong> A monophthong is a pure vowel with a relatively steady sound quality.</p><h2>9. Common JAMB Traps</h2><ul><li>Do not identify vowel sounds by spelling alone.</li><li>Do not confuse /ɪ/ with /iː/.</li><li>Do not confuse /ʊ/ with /uː/.</li><li>Remember that a diphthong is one syllable containing a changing vowel sound, not simply two written vowels.</li><li>Pay attention to weak vowels and the schwa.</li><li>Be aware that some vowel pronunciations vary between accents.</li></ul><h2>10. Revision Points</h2><ul><li>Vowel sounds are produced without a complete blockage of airflow.</li><li>Monophthongs are steady or pure vowel sounds.</li><li>Diphthongs involve movement between vowel positions.</li><li>English has short and long monophthongs.</li><li>The schwa /ə/ commonly occurs in unstressed syllables.</li><li>IPA symbols represent sounds rather than ordinary spelling.</li></ul><h2>11. Self-Assessment Questions</h2><ol><li>What is a vowel sound?</li><li>Differentiate between a vowel letter and a vowel sound.</li><li>Define a monophthong.</li><li>Define a diphthong.</li><li>Give five examples of short vowel sounds and words containing them.</li><li>Give five examples of long vowel sounds and words containing them.</li><li>What is the schwa sound?</li><li>Identify the vowel sound in each of these words: ship, sheep, book, food and time.</li></ol>"
